insert into public.budget_of_work (grade, subject, content_markdown, source_file)
values (
  4,
  'Science',
  $bow$# Grade 4 Science — Budget of Work

## Term 1 (Q1: Materials — Mixtures and Simple Machines)
Wk1-2: Describing and classifying mixtures (solid-solid, solid-liquid, liquid-liquid) found at home and school | Wk3-4: Differentiating homogeneous from heterogeneous mixtures using everyday examples (juice, salad, muddy water) | Wk5-6: Exploring ways of separating mixtures (sorting, filtration, decantation, evaporation) and identifying which method suits which mixture | Wk7-8: Investigating simple machines (lever, pulley, inclined plane, wheel-and-axle) and how each makes work easier | Wk9-10: Applying knowledge of mixtures and simple machines to a term project — designing a simple tool or separation method to solve a real household problem.

**Special Science enrichment:** Have the learner actually separate a real mixture at home (e.g., sand and water, or rice and beans) using a method they chose themselves, and explain in writing why that method worked better than an alternative they considered.

## Term 2 (Q2: Living Things and Their Environment — Systems)
Wk1-2: Describing the major organ systems of the human body (digestive, respiratory, circulatory) and their basic functions | Wk3-4: Explaining how these systems work together to keep the body healthy | Wk5-6: Describing the parts and functions of plant systems (roots, stems, leaves, flowers) in supporting growth and reproduction | Wk7-8: Investigating interactions between living things and their environment (food chains, habitats) | Wk9-10: Explaining how human activities can affect the health of body systems and the balance of an ecosystem, and proposing ways to promote health and environmental care.

**Special Science enrichment:** For Wk1-4, have the learner track their own simple daily habits (meals, water intake, physical activity) for several days and connect at least one habit to the health of a specific organ system studied, rather than only listing system functions from a diagram.

## Term 3 (Q3: Force, Motion, and Energy — Magnets and Electricity)
Wk1-2: Investigating the properties of magnets (attraction, repulsion, poles) using real objects | Wk3-4: Exploring magnetic and non-magnetic materials and their everyday uses | Wk5-6: Describing simple circuits and how electricity flows through a closed circuit to light a bulb | Wk7-8: Differentiating conductors from insulators of electricity through simple, guided investigation | Wk9-10: Applying knowledge of magnets and circuits in a term project (e.g., building a simple working circuit or a magnet-sorting device) and explaining the science behind it.

**Special Science enrichment:** Require the term project (Wk9-10) to include one design iteration — the learner builds a first version, tests it, identifies one thing that didn't work, and explains the specific change they made to fix it.

## Term 4 (Q4: Earth and Space — Weather, Soil, and the Sun)
Wk1-2: Describing different types of soil and their characteristics (sandy, clay, loam) and suitability for plant growth | Wk3-4: Investigating the water cycle and its connection to weather patterns | Wk5-6: Describing common weather instruments (thermometer, rain gauge, wind vane) and the data they measure | Wk7-8: Explaining the Sun's role as Earth's main source of light and heat, and its effect on weather and daily life | Wk9-10: Year-end integration — connecting soil, water cycle, weather, and the Sun's role in a project that explains how these factors affect farming or daily community life.

**Special Science enrichment:** During Wk5-6, have the learner build or use a simple real instrument (a basic rain gauge from a container, or a thermometer) to take actual daily readings for at least a week, then compare their real data to the general weather pattern described in class.$bow$,
  'https://www.deped.gov.ph/wp-content/uploads/MATATAG-Science-CG-Grade-4-and-7.pdf'
);
